From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 07:07:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: End the executor's transaction after errors that
 may roll it back

SQLite rolls back the whole transaction by itself when some statements
fail with SQLITE_FULL, SQLITE_IOERR, SQLITE_BUSY or SQLITE_NOMEM, and
only the failed statement when others do: a statement that may stop
half-way, such as a multi-row INSERT ... SELECT, has a statement journal
and keeps the transaction open. SQLite recommends answering these errors
with an explicit ROLLBACK
(https://sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html#response_to_errors_within_a_transaction).

The executors left that to the caller, which checked `in_transaction()`.
That works for the SQLite executor, which asks the connection, but not
for the RDB executor, which only knows whether it holds a transaction
handle. On the DAYU200, after a single-row INSERT failed with
RDB_E_SQLITE_FULL inside a transaction, the executor still reported the
transaction as open, the next INSERT on the transaction's connection ran
in autocommit and stayed in the database, and the rollback failed with
RDB_E_ERROR. The batch runner only escaped this because a failed write
ends the batch; a read failing with SQLITE_IOERR would have let the rest
of the batch write outside a transaction, and the database actor of the
next change keeps transactions open across requests.

Both executors now roll back and end the open transaction after a
statement or query fails with one of those four codes, so the caller
sees the same thing everywhere: `in_transaction()` is false, the
transaction's changes are gone and the error is returned. For the cases
where SQLite keeps the transaction open, this gives up the rest of it,
which every caller does anyway after a write fails.

RDB doesn't pass SQLite's code on for most failing queries: a failure
to prepare or step one comes back as RDB_E_STEP_RESULT_CLOSED, and
SQLITE_BUSY during a step, after RDB's retries, as
E_STEP_RESULT_QUERY_EXCEEDED. So the RDB executor ends the open
transaction after any query whose statement failed; a failure to read a
row's columns, or of the caller's row callback, leaves it alone.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 80 passed. DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- --no-default-features --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: 79 passed (1 documented skip); with both backends 130 passed (1 skip). The new executor_disk_full_ends_the_transaction fills the database with `PRAGMA max_page_count` inside a transaction, with a single-row INSERT (SQLite ends the transaction) and with a two-row INSERT ... SELECT (SQLite keeps it), and checks that the transaction ended, that commit fails and that only the row from before the transaction is left; without the change it fails on RDB for the first statement and on SQLite for the second. executor_failing_query_reports_the_transaction_state checks that after a failing query in a transaction the executor reports the transaction as open only if rolling it back still discards its writes. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free in the three configurations. WPT IndexedDB/ with the whole P0 series applied, as measured for "indexeddb: Run the SQLite and RDB backends as executors of one engine": host SpiderMonkey 3,224, host QuickJS 3,229 and DAYU200 3,222 passing subtests of 4,511, every unexpected result also seen with the baseline build; idbtransaction-oncomplete 60 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent found the problem with a probe on the DAYU200, wrote the fix and the test, and ran the test with and without the fix; after a review, it made the RDB executor also end the transaction after any failing query statement, added the test for that and measured again)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
